pmode_clock_readback_npu.log -- XRT's max_clock_frequency_mhz against power mode AND load,
varied in one sitting.

Desktop 2: Ryzen 7 8700G, Phoenix XDNA1 (NPU driver 32.0.20101.3760, firmware 1.5.5.391,
XRT 2.21.0), Windows 11. Read through tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.exe --json (the in-process
xrt::device::get_info<max_clock_frequency_mhz> and the platform report's power_mode, every
poll), with Windows' GPU-engine utilization for the NPU adapter in the same row, while
xrt-smi configure --pmode was stepped through all five modes under a 2048^3 bf16 GEMM hold
and again idle. 2026-09-07 23:51 (contaminated, kept) and 2026-09-08 (clean).

WHY
---
The 92cba62 merge flagged a contradiction as UNRESOLVED (docs/DECISIONS.md): the monitor
work read max_clock_frequency_mhz as a live clock -- 800 MHz idle, 1800 MHz with an active
context, power mode Default (xrt_api_live_clock_and_pdh_npu.log) -- while the clock-probe
work read the same query as a flat 800 in every power mode (clock_probe_npu.log, one fresh
pyxrt process per mode, nothing running on the device). The merge's hypothesis was that one
varied load at fixed mode and the other varied mode at no load. Nobody had varied both.

WHAT WAS RUN
------------
--- test_pmode_clock.sh (23:51 run) ---
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Power mode x load, one sitting: the monitor logs XRT's max_clock_frequency_mhz, the power
# mode and the engine utilization every 0.25 s while a 2048^3 bf16 GEMM hold runs and
# xrt-smi configure --pmode is stepped through all five modes; then the same five modes idle.
# Resolves the contradiction flagged in 92cba62 (live 800/1800 readback vs "flat 800 across
# all five power modes"). Restores --pmode default at the end and prints the mode read back.
cd /c/Users/<user>/PycharmProjects/ryzen-ai-xdna1-quantization || exit 1
T="$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp"
EXE=./tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.exe
SMI="/c/Windows/System32/AMD/xrt-smi.exe"
LOG="$T/pmode_events.txt"; : > "$LOG"
ev() { echo "$(date +%H:%M:%S.%N | cut -c1-12) $*" | tee -a "$LOG"; }
setmode() { out=$("$SMI" configure --pmode "$1" 2>&1 | tr -d '\r' | grep -v '^\s*$' | tail -2 | tr '\n' ' '); ev "pmode $1 -> $out"; }

$EXE --json --no-hwinfo --interval 0.25 --smi-interval 1 > "$T/pmode_json.txt" 2>&1 &
MON=$!
sleep 1
ev "hold start"
bash "$T/hold_npu.sh" > "$T/hold_npu7.txt" 2>&1 &
HOLD=$!
sleep 6
for m in powersaver balanced performance turbo default; do setmode $m; sleep 6; done
wait $HOLD; ev "hold end"
cat "$T/hold_npu7.txt"
sleep 2
ev "idle pass"
for m in powersaver balanced performance turbo default; do setmode $m; sleep 3; done
sleep 1
kill $MON 2>/dev/null; wait $MON 2>/dev/null
echo "--- power mode read back after restore:"; "$SMI" examine -r platform 2>&1 | grep -i "power mode"
python - "$T" <<'EOF'
import json, sys, os
from collections import OrderedDict
T = sys.argv[1]
rows = [json.loads(l) for l in open(os.path.join(T, "pmode_json.txt"), encoding="utf-8") if l.strip()]
print("polls:", len(rows))
g = OrderedDict()
for r in rows:
    mode = r["xrt_api"]["power_mode"]; util = r["windows"]["utilization_pct"]; clk = r["xrt_api"]["clock_mhz"]
    k = (mode, "busy" if util > 50 else "idle")
    g.setdefault(k, []).append((clk, util, r["time"]))
for (mode, state), v in g.items():
    clks = [c for c, _, _ in v]; utils = [u for _, u, _ in v]
    print("%-12s %-5s n=%3d  clock MHz min/max %4.0f/%4.0f  util mean %5.1f%%  %s .. %s" % (mode, state, len(v), min(clks), max(clks), sum(utils)/len(utils), v[0][2], v[-1][2]))
print("--- timeline: every change of (mode, clock)")
last = None
for r in rows:
    key = (r["xrt_api"]["power_mode"], r["xrt_api"]["clock_mhz"])
    if key != last:
        print("%s mode=%-12s clock=%4.0f util=%5.1f" % (r["time"], key[0], key[1], r["windows"]["utilization_pct"])); last = key
EOF

--- test_pmode_clean.sh (clean run; identical except a 0.1 s monitor and the idle pre-check) ---
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Clean repeat of test_pmode_clock.sh: the NPU must be otherwise idle (the 23:51 run overlapped
# another session's 128-stream wide_resnet101_2 run). The monitor runs at 0.1 s this time, so
# the run also answers "does a 10 Hz monitor plus a GEMM hold hang the device" on its own.
cd /c/Users/<user>/PycharmProjects/ryzen-ai-xdna1-quantization || exit 1
T="$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp"
EXE=./tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.exe
SMI="/c/Windows/System32/AMD/xrt-smi.exe"
LOG="$T/clean_pmode_events.txt"; : > "$LOG"
ev() { echo "$(date +%H:%M:%S.%N | cut -c1-12) $*" | tee -a "$LOG"; }
setmode() { out=$("$SMI" configure --pmode "$1" 2>&1 | tr -d '\r' | grep -v '^\s*$' | tail -2 | tr '\n' ' '); ev "pmode $1 -> $out"; }

echo "=== pre-check: hardware contexts on the device, and python processes ==="
"$SMI" examine -r aie-partitions 2>&1 | grep -vE '^\s*$' | tail -3
powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command "Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | Where-Object { \$_.Name -match 'python' } | Select-Object ProcessId, @{n='Started';e={\$_.CreationDate.ToString('HH:mm:ss')}}, @{n='Cmd';e={ if (\$_.CommandLine) { \$_.CommandLine.Substring(0, [Math]::Min(110, \$_.CommandLine.Length)) } }} | Format-Table -AutoSize -Wrap"
if "$SMI" examine -r aie-partitions 2>&1 | grep -q "No hardware contexts"; then echo "device idle: ok"; else echo "ABORT: a hardware context is live on the device"; exit 2; fi
ev "pmode before: $("$SMI" examine -r platform 2>&1 | grep -i 'power mode' | tr -s ' ')"

$EXE --json --no-hwinfo --interval 0.1 --smi-interval 1 > "$T/clean_pmode_json.txt" 2>&1 &
MON=$!
sleep 1
ev "hold start"
bash "$T/hold_npu.sh" > "$T/clean_hold.txt" 2>&1 &
HOLD=$!
sleep 6
for m in powersaver balanced performance turbo default; do setmode $m; sleep 6; done
wait $HOLD; ev "hold end"
cat "$T/clean_hold.txt"
sleep 2
ev "idle pass"
for m in powersaver balanced performance turbo default; do setmode $m; sleep 3; done
sleep 1
kill $MON 2>/dev/null; wait $MON 2>/dev/null
ev "pmode after: $("$SMI" examine -r platform 2>&1 | grep -i 'power mode' | tr -s ' ')"
echo "=== post-check ==="; "$SMI" examine -r aie-partitions 2>&1 | grep -vE '^\s*$' | tail -2
python - "$T" <<'EOF'
import json, sys, os
from collections import OrderedDict
T = sys.argv[1]
rows = [json.loads(l) for l in open(os.path.join(T, "clean_pmode_json.txt"), encoding="utf-8") if l.strip()]
per = [r["period_s"] for r in rows if r["period_s"] >= 0]
print("polls: %d  period mean %.4f min %.3f max %.3f s" % (len(rows), sum(per)/len(per), min(per), max(per)))
pids = OrderedDict()
for r in rows:
    for c in r["contexts"]:
        if c["listed_by_xrt_smi"]: pids.setdefault((c["pid"], c["process"]), [r["time"], r["time"], c["memory_mb"]])[1] = r["time"]
for (pid, name), (a, b, mem) in pids.items(): print("context pid %d %s %s .. %s  %s MB" % (pid, name, a, b, mem))
g = OrderedDict()
for r in rows:
    mode = r["xrt_api"]["power_mode"]; util = r["windows"]["utilization_pct"]; clk = r["xrt_api"]["clock_mhz"]
    k = (mode, "busy" if util > 50 else "idle")
    g.setdefault(k, []).append((clk, util, r["time"]))
for (mode, state), v in g.items():
    clks = [c for c, _, _ in v]; utils = [u for _, u, _ in v]
    print("%-12s %-5s n=%4d  clock MHz min/max %4.0f/%4.0f  util mean %5.1f%% (min %5.1f max %5.1f)  %s .. %s" % (mode, state, len(v), min(clks), max(clks), sum(utils)/len(utils), min(utils), max(utils), v[0][2], v[-1][2]))
print("--- timeline: every change of (mode, clock)")
last = None
for r in rows:
    key = (r["xrt_api"]["power_mode"], r["xrt_api"]["clock_mhz"])
    if key != last:
        print("%s poll %4d mode=%-12s clock=%4.0f util=%5.1f" % (r["time"], r["poll"], key[0], key[1], r["windows"]["utilization_pct"])); last = key
EOF

