FINDING
-------
max_clock_frequency_mhz is a live readback that tracks BOTH axes: with a context active it
reads the power mode's clock to the MHz the trace unit measured (1800 / 800 / 1028 / 1800 /
1800), and with no hardware context on the device it reads 800 in every mode. A context that
exists but sits idle holds the mode's clock: RUN A's idle pass read 1028 in balanced for ~2 s
until the other session's context (pid 16756, 0 % engine time) left the device; RUN B, with no
context, read 800 at once in every mode. So both earlier readings were
correct for what they varied -- the clock-probe's "800 in every mode" was taken idle, the
monitor's "800 -> 1800" was taken in Default -- and the merge's hypothesis ("tracks load but
ignores --pmode") is wrong: it tracks --pmode too. Consequences: the readback is a valid
busy-clock indicator for the monitor and HWiNFO (it is the core clock while work runs, not a
nameplate and not a cap); an idle reading says nothing about the mode; and a number taken in
balanced or powersaver is distinguishable after the fact from the monitor's --json rows.

--pmode turbo: the escape error is reproduced under load and idle, the mode applies regardless
(the platform report reads Turbo and the busy clock is 1800, as in performance), and the
default switch after it errors the same way and also applies. Under a running GEMM every mode
switch took effect within one 0.25 s poll and the kernel kept running through
powersaver/balanced/performance (RUN A, before the contention hang; RUN B, all five).

NOT TESTED
----------
- A VitisAI EP context (all of this is an IRON/XRT hardware context).
- The mode's clock with a context present but idle, deliberately: RUN A's 1028 in balanced
  came from another session's leftover context, not a controlled one.
- Whether turbo differs from performance in anything but the error: the readback and the
  trace unit both say 1800 / 1.80 GHz for both.
